==English==

===Etymology===
With a secondary frequentative suffix {{m|en|-er}}, ultimately pointing to {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hankōną}}, an iterative to {{m|gem-pro|*hanhaną||to hang}}. Related to {{cog|nl|hunkeren||to crave}}, which continues the {{glossary|zero-grade}} {{glossary|iterative}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhæŋkə(ɹ)/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-hanker.wav ‎|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æŋkə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[crave]], [[want]] or [[desire]].
#: {{ux|en|If you '''hanker''' for chocolate, you'll like this fudge recipe.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|volume=II|page=289|passage=“O ’tis true enough, faith. I cannot understand Farmer Boldwood being such a fool at his time of life as to ho and '''hanker''' after thik{{sic}} woman in the way ’a do, and she not care a bit about en.”}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Tramp Abroad|passage=“Baked, they were tough; and even boiled, they warn’t things for a hungry man to '''hanker''' after.”}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Sawyer Abroad|chapter=1|passage={{...|You see, when we three came back up the river in glory, as you may say, from that long travel, and the village received us with a torchlight procession and speeches, and everybody hurrah’d and shouted,}} it made us heroes, and that was what Tom Sawyer had always been '''hankering''' to be.}}
#* {{RQ:Maugham Of Human Bondage|chapter=LXI|passage=She bade him good-night and hurried down the road. Next day he took her in a little watch with a brooch to pin on her dress. She had been '''hankering''' for it.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2012-10-13|title=Very good, sir|work=The Economist|url=https://www.economist.com/international/2012/10/13/very-good-sir|issn=0013-0613|passage={{...}} the newly rich '''hanker''' after old aristocratic glitz.}}

====Usage notes====
* Usually used with {{m|en|for}}, as in the example above; {{m|en|after}} may also be used.

